WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  even before the pandemic rape  prosecutions were falling sharply  women like leslie not her real name are  often told by police or prosecutors that  evidence isn't strong enough  in her case even though there were two  other complainants against the alleged  perpetrator  how did they phrase it their exact words  were it's not that we don't believe you  because we do  don't feel bad but we don't think the  jury would take it to court  so the jury wouldn't believe it yeah how  did you feel  i felt frozen and my face kind of went  into my hands and i just started to cry  currently only three percent of adult  rape cases in england and wales  lead to a charge down from 13 percent  five years ago  the government says it's deeply ashamed  that victims are being failed  the rape review found the reason for the  decline includes an increase  in invasive requests for the victim's  personal data  delays in investigations and  inconsistent support for victims  kate waited three years for a trial the  cps only accepted her case  after two appeals in my letter it said  factors  included the fact that i'd sustained no  physical injuries and that there were no  witnesses  how do you feel about that how many  rapes are going to have a witness  i mean i don't have an exact figure but  off the top of my head probably not a  lot  it helps explain the shocking figures  that of 128  000 estimated rape victims a year only  43 000  report to the police resulting in only  three thousand prosecutions  and then just two thousand convictions  responding to the report the government  says it will ensure that police  investigations  switch focus from the credibility of the  victim to the behavior of the suspect  they will publish scorecards to assess  results  and if the police need to take a  victim's phone they should aim to return  it within 24 hours  currently it can take months the  government says they're also looking  to make the court process easier such as  allowing more victims to give  pre-recorded video evidence  i want all agencies  whether it's the police or the cps or  the court service to have  the interests of the victim very firmly  in mind  at every stage campaigners welcome the  changes but say  it's not enough we've seen quite a lot  of extending of pilots commitments to  consultations  not um not a lot of sort of quick  positive  um decisive action that will make a  difference swiftly  to victims and survivors the pandemic  has added problems  despite nightingale courts like this one  in nottingham being set up in hotels  there's increased disruption and delay  the day before the trial i got a phone  call to say it had been cancelled due to  the lack of court space  i was ready to get in the car and go all  packed totally geared up and ready to go  get a call saying it's been cancelled  the vast majority of cases end like  leslie's  without a jury even hearing the evidence  and the question is  are the government's changes radical  enough to make a significant difference  jason farrell sky news

